### Mini Issue Tracker: example requests
### Works with the VS Code "REST Client" extension or JetBrains HTTP Client.
### Change @host if you moved the API port (API_HOST_PORT).

@host = http://localhost:8000
@api = {{host}}/api/v1

### Liveness and readiness
GET {{host}}/health

###
GET {{host}}/ready

### Reference data: statuses, priorities, transitions, field limits
GET {{api}}/meta

###
GET {{api}}/users

### Create an issue (201 + Location header)
POST {{api}}/issues
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Checkout button unresponsive on Safari",
  "description": "Clicking 'Pay now' does nothing on Safari 18.",
  "priority": "high",
  "assignee_id": 1
}

### List: filters, search, sort and paging
GET {{api}}/issues?status=open&status=in_progress&q=checkout&sort=-priority&limit=10&offset=0

### Get one issue
GET {{api}}/issues/1

### Partial update: send the version you last read (optimistic locking)
PATCH {{api}}/issues/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "in_progress",
  "version": 1
}

### Same version again -> 409 VERSION_CONFLICT (someone else already changed it)
PATCH {{api}}/issues/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "A stale edit",
  "version": 1
}

### No version -> 422, telling you to GET the issue for its current version
PATCH {{api}}/issues/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "status": "resolved"
}

### Unassign
PATCH {{api}}/issues/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "assignee_id": null,
  "version": 2
}

### Delete (204)
DELETE {{api}}/issues/1

### --- Error cases -------------------------------------------------------------------

### 422: blank title, unknown priority, unknown field
POST {{api}}/issues
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "   ",
  "priority": "urgent",
  "reporter": "me"
}

### 422: invalid id
GET {{api}}/issues/abc

### 404: unknown issue
GET {{api}}/issues/999999

### 422: unknown sort field
GET {{api}}/issues?sort=colour

### 404 / 405 from the framework still use the error envelope
GET {{api}}/nope

###
DELETE {{host}}/health

### Search wildcards are literal: "%" only matches titles containing a percent sign
GET {{api}}/issues?q=%25

### Your request id is echoed back (and appears in the server logs)
GET {{api}}/issues/999999
X-Request-ID: my-trace-123
